<section>
  <section>
    <h1>Dynamic updates</h1>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What?</h2>
    <p>A user's action triggers a dynamic update within the same page.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New fields appear in a form</li>
      <li>A button expands new content (disclosure)</li>
      <li>An overlay appears</li>
      <li>Results are filtered</li>
      <li>A confirmation or error message appears</li>
      <li>Search results are shown</li>
      <li>Single page application</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Accessibility</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Is the dynamic update <strong>predictable</strong> for all users?
      </li>
      <li>
        How can we announce a dynamic update to a user who{' '}
        <strong>doesn't have a visual overview</strong> of the screen?
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Possible techniques</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Do nothing</li>
      <li>Follow ARIA design patterns</li>
      <li>Move the focus to the info that has appeared</li>
      <li>New info is contained within live region</li>
      <li>Add an explanation</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technique 0: do nothing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        When dynamic update occurs <strong>lower</strong> in the page, and the
        user does not have to know that right away.
      </li>
      <li>
        Example: when you indicate that you travel with children, new fields
        appear lower in the form to specify their age (
        <a target="tweede" href="https://www.booking.com/">
          Booking.com
        </a>
        )
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technique 1: ARIA design patterns</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Some{' '}
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/">
          ARIA design patterns
        </a>{' '}
        add semantic information that helps to understand dynamic updates.
      </li>
      <li>Tabs</li>
      <li>Carousel</li>
      <li>Disclosure = show/hide component</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: disclosure</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        = a button that shows or hides contents by expanding or collapsing it
      </li>
      <li>
        See article{' '}
        <a href="https://www.anysurfer.be/en/documentation/articles/detail/show-and-hide-content">
          Show and hide content{' '}
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        Can be done with <code is:raw>summary/details</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/">
          Disclosure design pattern
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>
      <code is:raw>aria-expanded</code>
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        = an ARIA attribute to be added to the button that expands/collapses
      </li>
      <li>To indicate its state</li>
      <li>
        It clarifies the function of the button and indicates that it is a
        disclosure.
      </li>
      <li>Aria-expanded="true" means "section is now visible"</li>
      <li>Aria-expanded="false" means "section is now hidden"</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a target="tweede" href="https://www.belgiantrain.be/en">
          Language choice SNCB
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a target="tweede" href="https://www.anysurfer.be/en">
          Menu in responsive version AnySurfer
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Attention</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Correct place</li>
      <li>Indicate correct status at all times</li>
      <li>Neutral label</li>
      <li>Correct hiding technique</li>
      <li>Logic source code order</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Correct place</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Add the <code is:raw>aria-expanded</code> attribute to the trigger (link
        or button).
      </li>
      <li>
        Don't add it to the container of the content that may be expanded.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Good example</h2>
    <pre><code is:raw>&lt;button class="main-menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false"&gt;
	Menu&lt;/button&gt;
	</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Bad example</h2>
    <p>Expanded FAQ question:</p>
    <pre><code is:raw>&lt;a data-toggle="collapse" href="#collapse01"&gt;
Question 1&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;div id="collapse01" class="panel-collapse"
aria-expanded="true"&gt;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Correct status</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code is:raw>aria-expanded</code> is present with correct value{' '}
        <strong>when the page loads</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>Don't add it after the status has changed.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Adapt status whenever it changes</strong>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Neutral label</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code is:raw>aria-expanded</code> indicates status
      </li>
      <li>Button label should not indicate an action</li>
      <li>Not OK: show menu or hide search function</li>
      <li>OK: menu or search</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Correct hiding technique</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Hide collapsed content with <code is:raw>display:none</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Content must also be hidden for screenreaders (avoid techniques like
        class="sr-only" or "visuallyhidden")
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Source code order</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>First the button that controls visibility</li>
      <li>Immediately followed by the content that may (not) be visible.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Bad example</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Menu in responsive version of{' '}
        <a target="tweede" href="https://www.fine-arts-museum.be/en">
          Fine Arts museum
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        Expanded content is in source code <strong>before</strong> the menu
        link.
      </li>
      <li>Screenreader and keyboard users won't go back.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
      The <code is:raw>aria-expanded</code> technique can be used if new info
      appears immediately after the button.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technique 2: focus management</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>When new information appears elsewhere on the page</li>
      <li>Actively move focus to that info</li>
      <li>
        Article:{' '}
        <a target="tweede" href="http://www.sitepoint.com/learning-to-focus/">
          learning to focus()
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Attention points with focus management</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Move focus to the container of the new content or to the very first
        element inside.
      </li>
      <li>
        You may need to add <code is:raw>tabindex="-1"</code> to the element you
        want the focus to move to.
      </li>
      <li>
        Loading new content via AJAX may take some time. Delay the focus
        movement with one second.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: show more</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          target="tweede"
          href="https://www.politie.be/5998/nl/zoeken?search_query=Federaal">
          Search results police website
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>After tenth search result follows a button "load more".</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Focus moves</strong> from "load more" button to beginning of new
        content.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Focus management is technique to be used when the user must interact
        with new content.
      </li>
      <li>Sometimes new info appears that one just needs to read.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technique 3: Live region</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>= an area on the screen where content may be added or updated</li>
      <li>Screenreader automatically reads the modified content</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: password strength</h2>
    <p>
      <img
        src="https://images.anysurfer.be/liveregions/aria_live_password_strength.png"
        alt="screenshot wachtwoordveld met sterkte (could be stronger)"
      />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: warning</h2>
    <p>
      <img
        src="https://images.anysurfer.be/liveregions/expired_QR_nl.png"
        alt="screenshot vervallen QR code met melding"
      />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Code</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code is:raw>aria-live="polite"</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code is:raw>aria-live="assertive"</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Polite waits until the screenreader is finished; Assertive interrupts
      </li>
      <li>
        <code is:raw>aria-live="off"</code>: container is temporarily not a live
        region.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Code</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Polite live region can also with <code is:raw>role="status"</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Assertive live region with <code is:raw>role="alert"</code>
      </li>
      <li>Alert possible on page load</li>
      <li>Screenreader prefixes with "alert".</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Attention</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Container where new info appears or changes, must already be marked as
        live region prior to the update.
      </li>
      <li>
        E.g. if you want to be able to add an error message to a form field, a
        container with <code is:raw>aria-live="polite"</code> must be present
        when the page loads. Later, you will inject the error message into that
        container.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Live region</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Only reads out loud; does not move focus</li>
      <li>Can't repeat new info</li>
      <li>
        Limit to short information: e.g. when filtering: only the changed amount
        of results found is live region, not the full results list
      </li>
      <li>You take control over the user's screenreader</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Don't use live regions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        When information changes frequently: e.g. time remaining counter for a
        video that is updated every second
      </li>
      <li>
        For information that is not important: e.g. Never use for a slide show
      </li>
      <li>
        When there are many automatic updates on a page: e.g. real time stocks
      </li>
      <li>Edit fields</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Live region can be a useful technique to attract user's attention to
        info that is added/changed/modified.
      </li>
      <li>Only when user does not need to interact with that information.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Technique 4: add explanation</h2>
    <p>If no authoring practice exists for the widget you're building:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Try to use previous techniques where apropriate</li>
      <li>Sometimes you may want to add (invisible) explanation</li>
      <li>
        Extra headings may be helpful to facilitate navigation between sections
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: dynamic search function</h2>
    <p>Invisible text before search field:</p>
    <blockquote>
      As soon as you have entered 5 characters in the search box, search results
      will appear under the heading "Publications found".
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: dynamic filter</h2>
    <p>
      Provide an "invisible" heading at the top of filter options and one at the
      top of the results section.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p>
      "Monitoring multiple dynamic updates remains a challenge for users who
      have no overview of their screen. E.g. a chat box.
    </p>
  </section>
</section>
